  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis, on ocrelizumab (G35.A)</content>. Good recovery from the relapse after steroids and occupational therapy: nine-hole peg times down from 24.5 and 31.0 to 20.5 and 23.0 seconds. The first infusion caused a mild infusion reaction, which is common with the first dose. Blood count and liver tests are normal and the glucose is normal after steroids. Urine pregnancy test negative today. [S]<list><item>Ocrelizumab 300 mg today, with premedication and the slower rate.</item><item>Hepatitis B vaccine dose 2 today.</item><item>Re-baseline MRI of the brain in about three months.</item><item>Continue occupational therapy; build practice up gradually.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency (E55.9)</content>. Taking cholecalciferol daily. [S]<list><item>Level in about two and a half months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count with lymphocytes</td><td>Before each ocrelizumab dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL, white cells 7.9, lymphocytes 1.6, platelets 252</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests and glucose</td><td>After high-dose steroids and before each dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>ALT 21 U/L, AST 22 U/L; glucose 90 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Pregnancy test</td><td>Before each ocrelizumab dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urine pregnancy test negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B, HIV and other screening before B-cell therapy</td><td>Before the first dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Reviewed at the diagnosis visit: no hepatitis B infection and not immune, so vaccination continues</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Infusion reactions</td><td>With each infusion</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild reaction with dose 1 (throat itch, flushing); premedication and a slower rate today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Vaccination</td><td>Complete series where possible</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hepatitis B dose 2 today; recombinant zoster dose 2 due in two to six months after dose 1</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Hand function: nine-hole peg test, both hands</td><td>At diagnosis, after a relapse, and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>20.5 seconds right, 23.0 seconds left</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy</td><td>Through recovery from the relapse</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Program started a week after the diagnosis visit</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Re-baseline MRI of the brain</td><td>Three to six months after starting a disease-modifying therapy</td><td>Due</td><td>Booked for about three months after the first dose</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>About three months after starting replacement</td><td>Due</td><td>With the re-baseline MRI visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and anxiety (PHQ-9, GAD-7)</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 5; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Fatigue: causes and management</td><td>Each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Less tired; pacing practice in short blocks with rests</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>MRI of the brain and spine within one week and a neurology diagnosis within three weeks. Outcome by 1973-03-23: MRI in three days, lumbar puncture in a week, diagnosis and consultation at two weeks [S]</item><item>High-efficacy disease-modifying therapy started within six weeks of diagnosis, after screening and vaccinations. Outcome by 1973-04-27: Ocrelizumab started three weeks after the diagnosis; PCV20, recombinant zoster and hepatitis B vaccines given first [S]</item><item>Nine-hole peg times back to within 10 percent of normal in both hands, and full practice resumed. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item><item>No evidence of disease activity at six months (no relapse, no new or enhancing MRI lesion, no disability progression). In progress; target 1973-10-12 [S]</item><item>25-hydroxyvitamin D of 30 ng/mL or more. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 10 throughout. In progress; target 1973-10-12 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Hepatitis B vaccine (adjuvanted, two-dose series), dose 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Rae-Grant A, et al. Practice guideline recommendations summary: Disease-modifying therapies for adults with multiple sclerosis. Neurology 2018;90(17):777-788 (PMID 29686116) (2018); Montalban X, et al. ECTRIMS/EAN Guideline on the pharmacological treatment of people with multiple sclerosis. Mult Scler 2018;24(2):96-120 (PMID 29353550) (2018); Multiple sclerosis in adults: management (NICE guideline NG220) (2022); Wattjes MP, et al. 2021 MAGNIMS-CMSC-NAIMS consensus recommendations on the use of MRI in patients with multiple sclerosis. Lancet Neurol 2021;20(8):653-670 (PMID 34139157) (2021); Hauser SL, et al. Ocrelizumab versus Interferon Beta-1a in Relapsing Multiple Sclerosis. N Engl J Med 2017;376(3):221-234 (PMID 28002679) (2017); Farez MF, et al. Practice guideline update summary: Vaccine-preventable infections and immunization in multiple sclerosis. Neurology 2019;93(13):584-594 (PMID 31462584) (2019)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Re-baseline MRI and review in about ten weeks; occupational therapy continues; call for fever, urinary symptoms or any new neurological symptom. Next scheduled visit 1973-07-13: three months on ocrelizumab: re-baseline MRI, B-cell depletion, fatigue, vitamin D and hand function. [S]</paragraph></text>

